In article <hqi8in$2r1$1@news.albasani.net>,
Ray Kulhanek  <kulhanek.5@wright.edu> wrote:
>Archaic, yes.  It uses pre-ansi prototypes, since it was written
>pre-ansi.  Somewhat obscure, too; there are a lot of functions and
>global structures that you need to know about before you'll be able
>to understand what a function does.  I doubt there's a best practices
>list out there that the Nethack source doesn't violate, and in
>particular a lot of the modern beliefs about object orientation and
>such being necessary for good good are ignored entirely.  And yet,
>there are 156,000 lines of code and no real show-stopping bugs.
>While the Nethack coding style is unusual, it apparently works.

Well, there's a lot of half-duplication and huge tables that would vanish
with object-oriented redesign (youhitmonster vs. monsterhitmonster comes to
mind).  There are a lot of special cases in the game, and adding anything
usually requires changes in at least 3 or 4 places.

I recall, probably 15 years ago, diving into this as a half-competent coder.
ctags was my friend (anything that allows you to jump quickly from usage
to definition is *mandatory* for this size of code).

The lack of bugs mostly mean a lot of people have spent a lot of time playing
nethack and fixing most problems... both in the code and in the game (I
remember versions where the nurse dance could be vastly abused, for 
instance).  Not that it is nice code.

It is an astoundingly good game, on the other hand...


